WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.870
At a mid-ocean ridge, two plates move apart.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.570
Mantle rises beneath the opening, and magma supplies new oceanic crust.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.990
Lava erupting into seawater forms pillow basalts.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:15.770
Magma that freezes inside narrow fractures forms dikes, while other magma

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.610
crystallizes deeper down. Normal faults let blocks move down toward the

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.030
rift. New crust is then carried away as spreading continues.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:29.790
The source compares broad, relatively smooth fast-spreading ridges with slower ridges

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.390
that have deep axial rifts.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:35.610
Faster magma supply and spreading can build a broad crest before

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.850
cooling causes subsidence. Slower crust can cool and strengthen enough for

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:43.190
larger normal faults. These are end members, not a rule that

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.610
every ridge has the same shape or one universal spreading rate.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:51.570
Away from the ridge, the plate cools, becomes denser and subsides.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.790
Sediment accumulates and can bury rough volcanic relief.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.150
Preservation also depends on chemistry:

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.410
below the local carbonate compensation depth, carbonate dissolves as fast as

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.350
it accumulates. The textbook gives roughly forty-five hundred to five thousand

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.470
meters, while explicitly noting that the actual depth varies.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.010
Clay and silica-rich material can remain.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.990
Cold seawater enters fractures, heats near hot rock and magma, and

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.030
leaches dissolved chemicals from the rock.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.050
Hot fluid rises and escapes into cold seawater.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.610
Mixing and cooling precipitate tiny sulfide minerals, making the dark plume

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.410
and building a chimney. This is mineral precipitation, not smoke from

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:33.970
burning. Deep-water pressure can keep the very hot vent fluid from

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:34.290
boiling.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:39.390
Lecture Fifteen connects black smokers with ore deposits and unusual ecology.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.650
No sunlight reaches these deep vents.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:46.230
Bacteria use chemical energy associated with substances such as hydrogen sulfide,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.490
supporting tube worms and an associated community of clams, crabs and

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.990
other animals. Keep the systems distinct:

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:58.450
magma supplies heat, seawater circulates, minerals precipitate, and chemical energy supports

00:01:58.450 --> 00:01:58.810
life.
